==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|frm|théorie}}, from {{der|en|LL.|theōria}}, from {{der|en|grc|θεωρία|t=contemplation, speculation, a looking at, things looked at}}, from {{m|grc|θεωρέω|t=I look at, view, consider, examine}}, from {{m|grc|θεωρός|t=spectator}}, from {{m|grc|θέα|t=view}} + {{m|grc|ὁράω|t=I see, look}} [i. e. θέαν ὁράω (théan horáō, “see, look at a view; survey + genitive”)].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθɪə.ɹi/|/ˈθiː.ə.ɹi/|/ˈθɪɹ.i/|a=Received Pronunciation,General Australian}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈθɪɚ.i/|/ˈθi.ə.ɹi/|a=General American}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-theory.ogg|a=US}}
*: {{rhymes|en|ɪəɹi|iːəɹi|s1=2|s2=3}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{senseid|en|description}} A description of an event or system that is considered to be accurate.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1646|author=w:Thomas Browne|title=Pseudodoxia Epidemica|section=VII.19
|passage=As they encrease the hatred of vice in some, so doe they enlarge the '''theory''' of wickednesse in all.}}
# {{senseid|en|sciences}} {{lb|en|sciences}} A [[coherent]] [[statement]] or set of ideas that [[explain]]s [[observe]]d [[fact]]s or [[phenomenon|phenomena]] and correctly predicts new facts or phenomena not previously observed, or which sets out the [[law]]s and principles of something known or observed; a [[hypothesis]] confirmed by observation, experiment etc. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* '''1843''', {{w|John Stuart Mill}}, ""A System of Logic, Ratiocinative and Inductive, ..., Bk V, Ch 7:
#*: In its most proper acceptation, '''theory''' means the completed result of philosophical induction from experience.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1990|author=Tony Bennett|title=Outside Literature|page=139|passage=Does this mean, then, that there can be no such thing as a '''theory''' of literature?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Duncan Steel|journal=The Guardian|date=23 May 2002
|passage=It was only when Einstein's '''theory''' of relativity was published in 1915 that physicists could show that Mercury's "anomaly" was actually because Newton's gravitational '''theory''' was incomplete.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2003|author=w:Bill Bryson|title=A Short History of Nearly Everything|publisher=BCA|page=118
|passage=The world would need additional decades [...] before the Big Bang would begin to move from interesting idea to established '''theory'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2009|author=w:Richard Dawkins|title=The Greatest Show On Earth: The Evidence for Evolution|page=10|publisher=Bantam
|passage=Scientists and creationists are understanding the word "'''theory'''" in two very different senses. Evolution is a '''theory''' in the same sense as the heliocentric '''theory'''. In neither case should the word "only" be used, as in "only a '''theory'''".}}
#* {{quote-journal
|1=en
|date=2012-01
|author=Michael Riordan
|title=Tackling Infinity
|volume=100
|issue=1
|page=86
|magazine=w:American Scientist
|url=http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/tackling-infinity
|passage=Some of the most beautiful and thus appealing physical '''theories''', including quantum electrodynamics and quantum gravity, have been dogged for decades by infinities that erupt when theorists try to prod their calculations into new domains. Getting rid of these nagging infinities has probably occupied far more effort than was spent in originating the '''theories'''.
|accessdate=12 May 2013
|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20130430074403/http://www.americanscientist.org/bookshelf/pub/tackling-infinity
|archivedate=30 April 2013
}}
# {{senseid|en|principles}} {{lb|en|uncountable}} The underlying [[principle]]s or [[method]]s of a given technical skill, [[art]] etc., as opposed to its [[practice]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1998|author=Elizabeth Souritz|title=The Great History of Russian Ballet|passage=Lopukhov wrote a number of books and articles on ballet '''theory''', as well as his memoirs.}}
# {{senseid|en|mathematics}} {{lb|en|mathematics}} A [[field]] of [[study]] attempting to [[exhaustive]]ly [[describe]] a particular [[class]] of [[construct]]s. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|Knot '''theory''' classifies the mappings of a circle into 3-space.}}
# {{senseid|en|hypothesis}} A [[hypothesis]] or [[conjecture]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Wes DeMott|title=Vapors|passage=It's just a '''theory''' I have, and I wonder if women would agree. But don't men say a lot about themselves when a short-skirted woman slides out of a car or chair?}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Sean Coughlan|journal=The Guardian|date=21 Jun 2003
|passage=The '''theory''' is that by stripping costs to the bone, they are able to offer ludicrously low fares.}}
# {{senseid|en|logic}} {{lb|en|countable|logic}} A [[set]] of [[axiom]]s together with all [[statement]]s [[derivable]] from them; ''or'', a set of statements which are {{w|Deductive closure|deductively closed}}. ''Equivalently,'' a [[formal language]] plus a set of [[axiom]]s (from which can then be derived [[theorem]]s). The statements may be required to all be bound (''i.e.'', to have no [[free variable]]s).
#: {{ux|en|A '''theory''' is consistent if it has a model.}}
# {{senseid|en|conception}} {{lb|en|obsolete}} Mental [[conception]]; [[reflection]], [[consideration]]. {{defdate|16th|18th c.}}

====Usage notes====
In scientific discourse, the sense “unproven conjecture” is discouraged (with {{m|en|hypothesis}} or {{m|en|conjecture}} preferred), due to unintentional ambiguity and intentional equivocation with the sense “well-developed statement or structure”.

